export const BEANS_MANUAL_MODULES: BeansManualModule[] = [
{ id:'module-1', title:'Introduction and background', page:'1', introduction:'Beans are a major food, nutrition and income crop for Ugandan households. This module explains the crop’s importance, the conditions it needs and the people and services involved in its value chain.', sections:[
{id:'1-1',title:'Importance of beans',content:'Common bean (Phaseolus vulgaris L.) is widely grown by smallholder farmers in Uganda. Beans provide protein and important micronutrients, including B vitamins, iron, calcium and zinc. They can be eaten as young pods, fresh mature grain or dry grain; leaves may also be used as food. Beans are an important source of household income, particularly for women and young people. Through root nodules containing compatible rhizobia, bean plants can fix atmospheric nitrogen. In suitable systems, beans can contribute to soil fertility and fit into rotations or intercrops. Their benefits depend on healthy crop establishment and good management.'},
{id:'1-2',title:'Ecological requirements',content:'The MAAIF manual describes a favourable temperature range of about 20–28°C, with temperatures below 15°C or above 32°C potentially reducing pod set. Beans generally perform best in reasonably fertile, well-drained and aerated soils. The manual gives an optimum soil pH range of about 5.8–6.5 and warns that strongly acidic conditions can limit production. Beans do not tolerate prolonged waterlogging. Rainfall distribution matters: excessive moisture late in the season can encourage pod rotting, while moisture stress during flowering and pod filling can reduce yield. These are general guides; variety, altitude, season and local field conditions matter.'},
{id:'1-3',title:'Challenges and opportunities',content:'Production constraints include poor agronomic practices, declining soil fertility, limited access to improved seed, mixed or low-quality planting seed, moisture stress, weeds, insect pests, diseases, post-harvest losses, weak storage facilities and unstable prices. Opportunities include demand in domestic and regional markets, improved varieties suited to different environments, agricultural research institutions, extension services and opportunities to sort, grade, process and market beans collectively. A farmer should assess both production risks and the buyer’s requirements before investing.'},
{id:'1-4',title:'Key value chain actors',content:'The bean value chain includes input suppliers, seed producers and multipliers, farmers, village assemblers and agents, aggregators, wholesalers, retailers, processors and consumers. Enabling actors include research organisations such as NARO and Makerere University, extension workers, NGOs, financial institutions, standards bodies and government agencies. Understanding who supplies inputs, who provides advice, who buys grain and what quality they require helps a farmer make better production and marketing decisions.'}
]},
{ id:'module-2', title:'Pre-planting and planting activities', page:'3', introduction:'Good Agricultural Practices begin before planting. Planning, field selection, soil assessment, seed quality, planting time and crop establishment influence the whole season.', sections:[
{id:'2-1',title:'2.1 Planning at household level',content:'Discuss the bean enterprise with the household before the season. Agree on the purpose (food, sale or both), the variety, area to plant, planting period, available labour, input budget and who will handle different tasks. Planning together helps avoid disagreements and makes it easier to organise labour, seed, staking materials and market arrangements. Write down the decisions and expected dates.'},
{id:'2-2',title:'2.2 Site selection',content:'Choose a field with deep, fertile, well-drained and aerated soil. Sandy loam or loam soils are generally suitable. The manual gives pH 5.8–6.5 as a favourable range and notes that beans are sensitive to strongly acidic soils. Avoid waterlogged areas, very sandy soils and compacted fields. Observe slope and erosion risk; on steep land, seek extension advice on contour-aligned hedgerows, terraces or other soil-conservation measures. Indicator plants can provide clues about field conditions but cannot replace a soil test.'},
{id:'2-3',title:'2.3 Soil testing',content:'A soil test helps identify acidity and nutrient deficiencies, estimate suitable fertiliser requirements for a production target and estimate input costs and likely returns. Soil pH and nutrient status can be assessed through a recognised laboratory or an appropriate field kit. Collect samples using instructions from an extension worker so that the sample represents the field. Keep results with the farm records. A photograph or visual inspection alone cannot measure exact pH or nutrient levels; mapped soil information is an estimate, not a laboratory result.'},
{id:'2-4',title:'2.4 Land preparation',content:'Prepare a workable seedbed that allows uniform planting, good seed-to-soil contact and root development. Field preparation may include clearing, removing obstacles, managing weeds and loosening the soil. A well-prepared field can make early weed management easier. Avoid unnecessary soil disturbance and protect the soil from erosion.'},
{id:'2-4-1',title:'2.4.1 Timing of land preparation',content:'The manual recommends beginning preparation after the previous harvest or at least about three weeks before planting. This gives plant residues time to break down and allows field operations to be organised. Where weeds have produced many seeds, early preparation can encourage a flush of germination that can be managed before planting. Fields with a history of bean pests may need careful field hygiene and locally advised preparation practices. Do not burn crop residues: burning destroys useful organic matter and nutrients and leaves soil exposed.'},
{id:'2-4-2',title:'2.4.2 Method of land preparation and related equipment',content:'Conventional methods include hand hoes, animal traction, walking tractors and tractors. Hand hoes are common for small plots but require considerable labour. Animal traction can be useful for suitable terrain and soil conditions but is not appropriate everywhere, especially on steep or very heavy wet soils. Walking tractors and larger tractors may suit larger areas where access and cost allow. Conservation tillage reduces the intensity of soil disturbance and retains crop residues or other cover. The manual describes at least 30% surface cover as a core conservation-tillage principle. Choose a method that protects soil structure, reduces erosion and allows timely planting.'},
{id:'2-4-3',title:'2.4.3 Application of manure',content:'The manual discusses incorporating compost or animal manure during early cultivation so it can decompose before planting. It gives a general range of 2–4 tonnes per acre, but actual suitability depends on manure quality, soil condition, previous applications and local recommendations. Do not treat this figure as a universal prescription. Use mature, well-decomposed manure and seek advice based on soil-test results. Avoid contaminated materials and keep manure handling hygienic.'},
{id:'2-5',title:'2.5 Source of quality seed',content:'Good seed is well developed, mature, uniform, clean, healthy and capable of producing vigorous seedlings. The manual recommends obtaining seed from certified seed companies or recognised Quality Declared Seed (QDS) producers. Check the variety name, supplier, lot and label details. Avoid mixed, mouldy, insect-damaged, shrivelled, broken, rotten or discoloured seed. Grain kept for food is not automatically suitable for planting.'},
{id:'2-5-1',title:'2.5.1 Choice of suitable variety',content:'Choose a variety based on the local altitude and climate, length of growing season, market preferences, intended use, yield potential and known tolerance or resistance to important pests and diseases. Bush beans and climbing beans have different growth habits; climbing types need support and are often suited to cooler highland settings, although suitability depends on the variety. Grain colour, size, cooking quality, nutritional traits and buyer demand also matter. Ask an extension worker or trusted seed supplier for current local recommendations rather than relying only on old variety tables.'},
{id:'2-5-2',title:'2.5.2 Attributes of quality beans seed',content:'The manual describes quality seed as uniform, clean, true to variety, well dried (around 13% moisture in its criteria), with high germination and minimal defects. It cites germination above 85% and high physical and varietal purity as important attributes. Seed should not be mouldy, rotten, faded, broken, shrivelled or damaged by insects. The precise acceptance standard depends on whether seed is certified or improved/QDS; refer to the applicable seed label and current standards.'},
{id:'2-5-3',title:'2.5.3 Steps in conducting a germination test',content:'The manual describes a simple check using a randomly selected sample of 100 seeds about a week before planting. Place seeds in clean moist sand in a container that drains. Keep the sand moist, not waterlogged. After about seven days, assess seedlings: normal seedlings have adequately developed roots and shoots; abnormal seedlings have weak or missing structures; some seeds may be rotten or fail to germinate. Record the counts and calculate the percentage of normal seedlings. The source manual provides an example of adjusting seed quantity when germination is between its stated thresholds; obtain extension or seed-supplier advice before changing a commercial seed rate. If germination is poor, report it promptly to the supplier or extension worker.'},
{id:'2-6',title:'2.6 Planting',content:'Plant when reliable soil moisture is available and the expected season can support the selected variety to maturity. The manual describes the first season commonly beginning around March to mid-April in many areas, with a second season varying by region. These are broad historical patterns, not fixed dates for every district or year. Use local rainfall information, current forecasts and extension advice. Avoid planting too late because the crop may face greater pest and disease pressure or fail to complete its growth cycle. In seasons with prolonged heavy rain, planting decisions should also consider the risk of wet conditions during pod filling and harvest.'},
{id:'2-6a',title:'Inoculation and nitrogen fixation',content:'Bean roots can form nodules with compatible rhizobia bacteria. These bacteria help convert atmospheric nitrogen into forms the plant can use. Inoculants are specific to particular legume crops and must be suitable for beans. The manual advises checking that the correct bean inoculant is used and following the product’s approved instructions. Nodules do not guarantee that all nutrient needs are met; soil acidity, moisture, phosphorus and other conditions affect nodulation. Seek extension advice before using an inoculant.'},
{id:'2-7',title:'2.7 Spacing and seed rate (population density)',content:'Spacing affects plant population, competition, airflow, weed control and yield. The manual includes different recommendations for bush and climbing bean types and for sole cropping or intercropping. Do not apply one spacing to every variety or field. Follow the variety-specific seed guidance and current local extension recommendations. Mark rows consistently so that plants are evenly distributed and field operations are easier.'},
{id:'2-8',title:'2.8 Planting depth',content:'Place seed at a consistent depth in moist soil and cover it gently. Planting too shallow can expose seed to drying, birds or displacement; planting too deep can delay or prevent emergence, especially in heavy soils. The suitable depth depends on soil texture, moisture and planting method. Follow current local guidance for the selected variety and field conditions.'},
{id:'2-9',title:'2.9 Methods of planting and related equipment',content:'Beans may be planted by hand in marked rows or planting stations, using simple tools and string to maintain straight rows. Larger operations may use suitable planters. Ensure seed is placed consistently and is not crushed or left exposed. Clean planting equipment between fields where disease spread is a concern. Choose a method that fits the farm scale and allows the recommended plant population to be achieved.'},
{id:'2-10',title:'2.10 Basal fertilizer application',content:'Fertiliser decisions should be guided by soil testing, field history, variety and local extension recommendations. Beans can fix some nitrogen when effective nodulation occurs, but they still require other nutrients and may not nodulate well in poor soil conditions. Avoid assuming that beans need no fertiliser or applying fertiliser blindly. The manual discusses basal fertiliser as part of establishment; use only a locally recommended product and method.'}
]},
{ id:'module-3', title:'Crop management practices', page:'12', introduction:'After emergence, the crop needs timely field care. This module covers climbing-bean support, weeds, soil fertility, pests and diseases, with prevention and monitoring as the foundation.', sections:[
{id:'3-1',title:'3.1 Staking of climbing beans',content:'Climbing bean varieties grow vertically and need suitable support. Stakes or other locally appropriate structures help plants climb, improve light exposure and make field access and harvesting easier. Plan and obtain staking materials before the plants begin vigorous climbing. Place supports securely without damaging roots or stems. Bush varieties generally do not require staking; confirm the growth habit of the selected variety.'},
{id:'3-2',title:'3.2 Weed management',content:'Weeds compete with beans for light, water, nutrients and space. Heavy weed growth can reduce flowering and pod production, interfere with harvesting and provide shelter for pests. Control is most important while the crop is establishing and before weeds set seed. Combine prevention, timely hand weeding, suitable cultivation and other locally recommended practices.'},
{id:'3-2-1',title:'3.2.1 Weeds and their effects on beans',content:'Weeds reduce crop growth by competing for resources and may host pests or diseases. Some weeds produce many seeds that remain in the soil and create repeated problems in later seasons. Weeds can also make harvesting difficult and contaminate harvested grain. Identify the dominant weeds in the field and record where they occur.'},
{id:'3-2-2',title:'3.2.2 Categories of weeds',content:'Annual weeds complete their life cycle within one season and often spread through large numbers of seeds. Perennial weeds survive for more than one season and may regrow from roots or other plant parts. Parasitic weeds attach to host plants and can cause serious damage. Correct identification matters because a method that controls one weed may not control another.'},
{id:'3-2-3',title:'3.2.3 Methods of weed control',content:'Preventive practices include clean planting seed, clean tools, timely land preparation, crop rotation and preventing weeds from producing seed. Hand pulling and hoeing are common options for small farms; weed carefully to avoid damaging bean roots. Mulching and suitable crop arrangements can suppress weeds where appropriate. Mechanical or chemical options require local technical advice. This learning guide does not provide pesticide rates or application instructions.'},
{id:'3-3',title:'3.3 Improvement and management of soil fertility',content:'Healthy soil supports root growth, water supply, nodulation and pod development. Soil fertility management combines soil testing, organic matter, crop rotation, erosion control and locally appropriate fertiliser use. Assess the field rather than assuming every plot has the same nutrient needs.'},
{id:'3-3-1',title:'3.3.1 Causes of soil nutrient losses and soil infertility',content:'Nutrients can be lost through erosion, runoff, leaching, continuous cropping without nutrient replacement, burning residues and removal of all crop biomass. Soil compaction, poor drainage and strong acidity can also limit nutrient uptake even where some nutrients are present. Protect soil cover, manage water flow and return suitable organic matter.'},
{id:'3-3-2',title:'3.3.2 Essential plant nutrients',content:'Plants need major nutrients such as nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium, as well as secondary and micronutrients. Nitrogen supports leafy growth and is partly supplied to beans through effective biological fixation. Phosphorus supports root development and energy transfer, while potassium contributes to water regulation and general plant function. Sulphur, calcium, magnesium and micronutrients are also important. A soil test helps identify which nutrients may be limiting.'},
{id:'3-3-3',title:'3.3.3 Signs of nutrient deficiency in beans',content:'Nutrient problems may appear as poor growth, unusual leaf colour, weak roots, poor flowering or low pod formation. Different deficiencies can look similar to drought, waterlogging, pests or disease. Observe which leaves are affected, the pattern across the field and the crop stage. Do not diagnose a nutrient deficiency from leaf colour alone; use soil testing and extension support.'},
{id:'3-3-4',title:'3.3.4 Soil fertility management',content:'Combine soil-test-based nutrient decisions with erosion control, organic matter management, rotation and appropriate crop residues. Beans may contribute nitrogen through nodulation, but they remove other nutrients in harvested grain and residues. Plan to maintain soil fertility over several seasons rather than focusing only on one crop.'},
{id:'3-3-4-1',title:'3.3.4.1 Organic manure',content:'Well-decomposed animal manure and compost can add organic matter and nutrients, improve soil structure and support water retention. Nutrient content varies widely with source and handling. Use mature material, apply it in a way that avoids contaminating edible produce and seek advice on suitable quantities for the field.'},
{id:'3-3-4-2',title:'3.3.4.2 Inorganic fertilizers',content:'Mineral fertilisers can supply nutrients where soil tests or local recommendations identify a need. The right product and amount depend on soil condition, crop variety, previous applications and expected yield. Beans’ ability to fix nitrogen does not remove the need to manage phosphorus, potassium and other nutrients. Follow a qualified local recommendation and product label; do not invent or copy rates from a different farm.'},
{id:'3-3-5',title:'3.3.5 Other methods to manage soil fertility',content:'Use crop rotation, suitable intercropping, mulching, cover crops, erosion-control structures and retention of healthy crop residues. Avoid burning residues and minimise unnecessary soil disturbance. Rotate beans with suitable non-legume crops to help manage pests and diseases and diversify nutrient demand.'},
{id:'3-4',title:'3.4 Field pests and disease management',content:'Use integrated pest and disease management: begin with prevention, inspect the field regularly, identify the likely cause and choose a response based on evidence. Healthy seed, suitable varieties, rotation, field hygiene, appropriate spacing and good drainage reduce several risks. Similar symptoms can have different causes, so a photo or one symptom is not always enough for a diagnosis.'},
{id:'3-4-1',title:'3.4.1 Major bean insect pests and their management',content:'The manual discusses important bean insect pests including bean fly, aphids, foliage-feeding insects, pod borers and beetles. Damage may include seedling wilting or stem injury, curled leaves, sap-feeding symptoms, holes in leaves or pods and damaged grain. Scout at different crop stages and inspect the underside of leaves, growing points, stems and pods. Record the affected area and crop stage. Encourage natural enemies, use clean seed and appropriate field hygiene, and seek extension confirmation before choosing a control. Do not assume every damaged leaf requires a chemical treatment.'},
{id:'3-4-2',title:'3.4.2 Major bean diseases and associated control measures',content:'The manual covers important bean diseases such as anthracnose, angular leaf spot, rust, common bacterial blight, bean common mosaic virus and root rots. Signs can include leaf spots, angular lesions, rust-coloured pustules, mosaic patterns, stem or pod lesions, wilting and root decay. Use disease-free seed, suitable locally recommended varieties, rotation, good drainage, appropriate spacing and field sanitation. Avoid working through wet foliage when disease spread is a concern. Confirm uncertain cases with an extension worker or diagnostic service; do not treat a suspected disease solely from a photograph.'}
]},
{ id:'module-4', title:'Safe handling and use of agrochemicals', page:'27', introduction:'Crop protection products can create health and environmental risks if selected, stored or handled incorrectly. This module is presented as safety awareness, not a substitute for trained adult supervision or product-label instructions.', sections:[
{id:'4-1',title:'4.1 Groups of agro-chemicals',content:'Agricultural chemicals include products intended to manage insects, diseases, weeds and other crop problems. Different products have different purposes and hazards. A product intended for one crop or pest must not be assumed suitable for another.'},
{id:'4-1-1',title:'4.1.1 Groups of agro-chemicals',content:'The manual groups products by their intended target, such as insect pests, fungal diseases and weeds. Product labels identify the active ingredient, approved crop and target, hazard warnings and restrictions. Classification and registration can change, so use current official information rather than relying on an old list.'},
{id:'4-1-2',title:'4.1.2 Advantages and limitations',content:'When correctly selected and used by trained adults according to current requirements, crop-protection products may help manage a confirmed problem. They can also be costly and may harm people, livestock, beneficial organisms, water and the environment when misused. Prevention, scouting and non-chemical methods should be considered as part of integrated management.'},
{id:'4-2',title:'4.2 Safe use of agro-chemicals',content:'Only trained and appropriately equipped adults should handle or apply agricultural chemicals. Keep children and other unauthorised people away. Never transfer products into food or drink containers. Follow current laws, label requirements and advice from qualified extension personnel.'},
{id:'4-2-1',title:'4.2.1 Identification of the pest',content:'Before any crop-protection decision, identify the pest, disease or weed accurately. Similar symptoms can have different causes, and unnecessary treatment wastes money and can create avoidable risk. Seek help from a qualified extension worker or diagnostic service when uncertain.'},
{id:'4-2-2',title:'4.2.2 Buying agro-chemicals',content:'The manual advises obtaining products only through licensed and registered agro-input dealers, checking original packaging and label information, and keeping proof of purchase. Current registration status and product authenticity should be checked through the appropriate official channels. Never buy an unlabelled, leaking, repackaged or suspicious product.'},
{id:'4-2-3',title:'4.2.3 Transporting agro-chemicals',content:'Agricultural chemicals must be kept sealed in their original containers and separated from food, feed, people and animals. Transport should prevent leaks and spills. If a container is damaged, do not handle the spill yourself; keep others away and seek qualified assistance.'},
{id:'4-2-4',title:'4.2.4 Storing agro-chemicals',content:'Store products only in their original labelled containers in a secure, designated place that is locked and inaccessible to children, food, feed and household items. Follow the label’s storage conditions. Do not store products in bedrooms, kitchens or ordinary household cupboards.'},
{id:'4-3',title:'4.3 Application of agro-chemicals',content:'This section is a safety reminder only. Application decisions and handling must be made by trained adults using the exact current product label and qualified local advice. Omulimi does not provide mixing, dosage or spraying instructions here.'},
{id:'4-3-1',title:'4.3.1 Reading the product label',content:'A label contains the product identity, approved uses, hazard symbols, protective requirements, first-aid information, environmental warnings, restricted-entry and pre-harvest intervals, storage and disposal directions. If any instruction is unclear, do not proceed; ask a qualified extension worker or authorised supplier.'},
{id:'4-3-2',title:'4.3.2 Determining how much pesticide to use',content:'Never estimate, guess or copy a pesticide quantity from another farm. The correct use, if legally permitted, is determined by the current approved label and qualified professional guidance. This guide intentionally does not reproduce application rates.'},
{id:'4-3-3',title:'4.3.3 Mixing agro-chemicals',content:'Do not mix products unless the current label explicitly permits it and a qualified professional confirms the combination. Mixing can create dangerous exposure, crop injury or environmental harm. This guide does not provide mixing procedures.'},
{id:'4-3-4',title:'4.3.4 Before spraying',content:'A trained adult must check the product label, legal approval, weather restrictions, protective requirements, nearby people, animals, water sources and any required exclusion period. If suitable protection or safe conditions are unavailable, do not proceed.'},
{id:'4-3-5',title:'4.3.5 During spraying',content:'Children and bystanders must stay away from chemical application. A trained adult must follow the product label and local safety requirements, avoid exposing other people or animals and stop if equipment leaks or conditions become unsafe.'},
{id:'4-3-6',title:'4.3.6 After spraying',content:'Observe the label’s restricted-entry and pre-harvest intervals. Keep people and animals away for the required period. Record the product and date in the farm records and seek professional advice if exposure or an unexpected reaction occurs.'},
{id:'4-3-7',title:'4.3.7 Disposal of empty containers',content:'Never reuse pesticide containers for water, food, fuel or household storage. Follow the label and official local disposal arrangements. Do not burn, bury or dump containers in water sources or ordinary public spaces unless an authorised procedure specifically directs their handling.'},
{id:'4-3-8',title:'4.3.8 Cleaning equipment and yourself',content:'Equipment decontamination and personal hygiene must follow the exact label and qualified safety instructions. Do not allow children to clean contaminated equipment or clothing. If exposure is suspected, follow the label’s first-aid directions and seek urgent medical assistance.'}
]},
{ id:'module-5', title:'Harvesting and post-harvest handling', page:'32', introduction:'Harvest timing, drying, threshing, cleaning and storage determine how much of the crop remains safe and marketable. Keep grain clean and protect it from moisture and pests.', sections:[
{id:'5-1',title:'5.1 Harvesting',content:'Harvest at suitable maturity for the intended product. Dry-bean harvest generally begins when most pods have matured and changed colour, while avoiding unnecessary delay that can expose pods to rain, shattering, pests or field mould. Harvest timing varies by variety and weather. Keep harvested material off bare soil and away from animals.'},
{id:'5-1-1',title:'5.1.1 Methods of harvesting',content:'Beans may be harvested by picking mature pods or uprooting/cutting plants, depending on the crop type and local practice. Handle plants carefully to reduce grain loss and contamination. Keep immature, diseased or wet material separate where appropriate.'},
{id:'5-2',title:'5.2 Postharvest handling',content:'Post-harvest handling includes collection, transport, drying, threshing, further drying, cleaning, sorting and storage. Plan a clean drying area and storage facility before harvest. Protect beans from rain, soil, animals, smoke, fuel and other contaminants.'},
{id:'5-2-1',title:'5.2.1 Transportation',content:'Transport harvested pods or grain in clean containers or on clean coverings. Avoid mixing beans with soil, stones, chemicals, animal feed or waste. Protect produce from rain and unnecessary delays, and keep damaged or suspect material separate.'},
{id:'5-2-2',title:'5.2.2 Drying of beans in the pods',content:'Dry pods on a clean tarpaulin, raised platform or other hygienic surface with good airflow. Protect them from rain, dew, animals and contamination. Turn material as appropriate so that drying is even. Do not dry directly on bare soil or on roads where dust, stones and vehicle contamination are likely.'},
{id:'5-2-3',title:'5.2.3 Threshing of beans',content:'Thresh only when pods are sufficiently dry. Use a clean method that minimises broken grain and contamination. Avoid excessive beating or trampling that damages seed coats. Separate grain from pods and foreign material promptly.'},
{id:'5-2-4',title:'5.2.4 Drying threshed grain',content:'After threshing, spread grain in a thin layer on a clean surface and protect it from rain, animals and dust. Turn it to promote even drying. Grain that feels dry may still contain too much moisture for safe storage; use a suitable moisture meter where available and follow buyer or storage standards.'},
{id:'5-2-5',title:'5.2.5 Methods of checking moisture content',content:'Moisture meters provide a more reliable measurement than judging by touch or sound. Where a meter is unavailable, traditional checks may offer only a rough indication and should not be treated as exact. Check several samples from different parts of the batch because moisture may be uneven. Store only grain that meets the relevant safe-storage or buyer moisture requirement.'},
{id:'5-2-6',title:'5.2.6 Grain cleaning',content:'Remove stones, soil, plant parts, broken or shrivelled grains, insect-damaged beans and other foreign matter. Winnowing and sieving can help separate lighter material and impurities. Use clean equipment and surfaces. Keep different varieties or quality grades separate when buyers require them.'},
{id:'5-2-7',title:'5.2.7 Storage',content:'Store dry, clean grain in a clean, dry, well-ventilated and pest-resistant store. Use suitable food-grade bags or approved hermetic storage systems according to their instructions. Keep bags on pallets, away from walls and damp floors, and protect the store from roof leaks and rodents. Inspect stocks regularly and keep records of variety, quantity, date and condition.'},
{id:'5-3',title:'5.3 Storage pests, mycotoxins and their control',content:'Storage insects and poor storage conditions reduce weight, seed quality, food safety and sale value. Moisture and mould can also create mycotoxin risks. Prevention starts with clean, dry grain and a hygienic store.'},
{id:'5-3-1',title:'5.3.1 Pest control',content:'Inspect stored beans for live insects, holes, powder, webbing, unusual odours and damaged grain. Check bags and store structures. Keep new grain separate from old infested stocks and clean the store before loading a new harvest.'},
{id:'5-3-2',title:'5.3.2 Control of pests',content:'Use prevention first: dry grain properly, clean the store, seal entry points, use suitable storage containers and inspect regularly. Where a pest problem persists, seek advice from a qualified storage or extension specialist. Do not use unlabelled products or improvised chemical treatments on food grain.'},
{id:'5-3-3',title:'5.3.3 Mycotoxins',content:'Mycotoxins are harmful substances that can be produced by certain moulds under favourable conditions. Damp, damaged or mouldy grain is a warning sign. Good drying, clean handling, protection from rain and moisture-proof storage reduce risk. Do not mix visibly mouldy or suspect grain into clean food or feed. Seek official food-safety advice on disposal and testing.'}
]},
{ id:'module-6', title:'Value addition', page:'41', introduction:'Value addition can improve convenience, market options and the value received from beans. Product quality, hygiene, equipment and buyer demand must guide investment.', sections:[
{id:'6-1',title:'6.1 Cleaning and sorting of bean grain',content:'Cleaning and sorting remove foreign matter and separate grain by size, colour, variety and visible quality. Uniform, clean grain is easier to package and may meet buyer requirements more consistently. Use clean surfaces and equipment, and keep different grades separate.'},
{id:'6-2',title:'6.2 Milling',content:'Beans can be milled into flour or other products. Milling requires suitable equipment, safe handling, clean raw material, hygienic processing and packaging that protects the product. Assess local demand, equipment costs, energy, maintenance, food-safety requirements and the expected selling price before investing.'},
{id:'6-3',title:'6.3 Biofortified beans',content:'Biofortified bean varieties are developed to provide higher levels of selected nutrients, particularly iron and zinc. The manual refers to varieties released through NARO and other research partners. Use genuine seed of a suitable released variety and preserve its identity during production, storage and marketing. Check current variety information because releases and availability can change.'},
{id:'6-4',title:'6.4 Pre-cooked beans',content:'Pre-cooked beans are processed to reduce the time and fuel needed by consumers. Production requires reliable processing methods, food-safety controls, hygienic packaging, quality testing and a clear market. Farmers or groups should seek technical support before setting up a processing operation.'}
]},
{ id:'module-7', title:'Marketing of beans', page:'42', introduction:'Successful bean marketing begins before planting. Understand the product buyers want, compare channels and organise the harvest to meet quality, quantity and timing requirements.', sections:[
{id:'7-1',title:'7.1 Bean products',content:'Bean products include dry grain, fresh mature beans, green pods, leaves where consumed, flour and processed or pre-cooked products. Market opportunities depend on local food habits, processing capacity, variety, grain colour and size, cooking quality and nutrition traits.'},
{id:'7-2',title:'7.2 Access to markets',content:'Identify potential buyers before harvest: local consumers, traders, wholesalers, retailers, institutions, processors or group buyers. Ask about preferred variety, quantity, quality, packaging, delivery time and payment terms. Compare prices after accounting for transport, handling, storage and transaction costs.'},
{id:'7-3',title:'7.3 Entrepreneurial skills and marketing',content:'Treat bean production as a business. Understand customers, estimate costs, calculate the break-even point, compare selling options and negotiate using reliable market information. Keep records of purchases, labour, harvest quantities, sales, transport and payments. Do not judge profitability from sales revenue alone; subtract all production and marketing costs.'},
{id:'7-4',title:'7.4 Group and collective marketing',content:'Farmers may bulk produce through a group or cooperative to assemble larger quantities and negotiate with buyers. Collective marketing requires trust, clear rules, transparent weighing and grading, reliable records and agreed responsibilities.'},
{id:'7-4-1',title:'7.4.1 Group and collective marketing in beans business',content:'Members agree on production plans, quality standards, delivery dates, aggregation arrangements and how sales proceeds and costs will be handled. The group should document each member’s quantity and quality and issue clear receipts.'},
{id:'7-4-2',title:'7.4.2 Advantages of collective marketing',content:'Collective marketing can increase bargaining power, reduce per-unit transport costs, make larger orders possible, improve access to market information and help members meet buyer requirements. Benefits depend on sound management and reliable group participation.'},
{id:'7-4-3',title:'7.4.3 Challenges of collective marketing',content:'Challenges include delayed delivery, inconsistent quality, mistrust, weak leadership, poor records, disagreements over prices or payments, side-selling and lack of working capital. Clear rules and transparent communication help reduce these risks.'},
{id:'7-4-4',title:'7.4.4 Key success factors in group marketing',content:'The manual highlights business knowledge, regular participation, mutual trust, accountable elected leadership, clear by-laws, defined roles, access to finance and market information, pre-production planning, uniform product quality, safe storage, good records and fair distribution of benefits.'},
{id:'7-5',title:'7.5 Other marketing channels',content:'Other channels described in the manual include contract farming, commodity exchanges and warehouse receipt systems. A warehouse receipt records grain deposited in a certified warehouse and may help a farmer access storage, quality assurance and finance. Contract farming is an agreement between a buyer and producer on quantity, quality, timing and other conditions. Read agreements carefully and understand fees, grading, delivery and payment terms before committing.'},
{id:'7-6',title:'7.6 Support services in beans marketing',content:'Marketing support may include extension, market information, transport, storage, finance, quality testing, aggregation, packaging and business development services. Verify providers and understand costs. Keep written records of agreements and payments.'}
]},
{ id:'module-8', title:'Beans grain standards', page:'49', introduction:'Standards help buyers and consumers receive grain that is safe, clean and consistent. Good handling and clear information protect market access.', sections:[
{id:'8-1',title:'8.1 Importance of grain standards',content:'Grain standards create a common basis for judging quality, support fair trade, reduce disputes and protect consumers. Buyers may reject grain that is damp, dirty, mixed, damaged or incorrectly labelled. Learn the current standard required by the intended buyer.'},
{id:'8-2',title:'8.2 Specifications',content:'Specifications can cover moisture, foreign matter, damaged or discoloured grain, insect damage, variety identity, size, packaging and other quality characteristics. The exact limits depend on the applicable current East African or Uganda standard and the buyer’s contract. Check the official standard rather than relying on an old table.'},
{id:'8-3',title:'8.3 Hygiene',content:'Keep grain, equipment, bags, drying surfaces, stores and transport clean. Keep produce away from chemicals, animals, waste, fuel and contaminated water. Workers should use clean hands and suitable protective practices during sorting and packing.'},
{id:'8-4',title:'8.4 Packaging',content:'Use clean, dry, strong and suitable food-grade packaging that protects grain during handling and transport. Select bag size and material according to buyer and standard requirements. Do not reuse chemical containers or contaminated bags for food.'},
{id:'8-5',title:'8.5 Labelling',content:'Labels should accurately identify the product and provide information required by the applicable standard or buyer, such as variety or product type, net weight, producer or packer details, lot or batch identification and relevant dates. Never make unsupported claims about nutritional content or certification.'}
]},
{ id:'module-9', title:'Farming as a business', page:'52', introduction:'A bean enterprise should be planned, costed, monitored and reviewed. Records help a farmer understand whether the enterprise is profitable and what to change next season.', sections:[
{id:'9-1',title:'9.1 Commercial farming',content:'Commercial farming means producing with a clear market and income objective while managing costs, quality, timing and risk. A farmer can produce for household food and still manage part of the crop as a business. Know the quantity likely to be sold and the quality buyers expect.'},
{id:'9-2',title:'9.2 Principles of business',content:'A business needs customers, a product that meets their needs, resources, reliable operations, cost control, records and a plan for earning more than it spends. Treat time, labour, land, seed and equipment as resources with costs, even when family labour is used.'},
{id:'9-3',title:'9.3 Farm planning and decision making',content:'Set a production objective, estimate the area, select a variety, prepare an activity calendar, budget for inputs and labour, identify likely buyers and plan harvest handling. Review weather, market and field information during the season and adjust decisions when necessary.'},
{id:'9-4',title:'9.4 Risk management',content:'Risks include drought, excessive rain, pests, diseases, poor seed, labour shortages, price changes, storage losses and buyer default. Reduce risk through suitable varieties, planting-time planning, crop rotation, field scouting, safe storage, more than one buyer where practical and clear payment agreements. No single measure removes every risk.'},
{id:'9-5',title:'9.5 Farm record keeping',content:'Keep records of land preparation, seed and input purchases, labour, field operations, crop problems, harvest quantity, storage, sales, prices, buyers and payments. Useful records include a farm calendar, input register, labour record, sales record, cash-flow statement and simple profit calculation. Record information as it happens rather than relying on memory.'},
{id:'9-6',title:'9.6 Resource mobilization and management',content:'List the land, labour, seed, tools, equipment, finance, transport and storage needed. Identify which resources are already available and which must be hired or purchased. Compare the cost and timing of different options and avoid borrowing without a realistic repayment plan.'},
{id:'9-7',title:'9.7 Formalisation of business',content:'A farmer group or enterprise may benefit from clear membership, leadership, records, agreed rules and any registration or licensing required for its activities. Requirements depend on the type and scale of business. Seek current guidance from the relevant Ugandan authority before formalising.'},
{id:'9-8',title:'9.8 Writing a business plan',content:'A simple business plan explains the product, customers, production plan, required resources, costs, expected sales, risks and how the enterprise will be managed. Include realistic assumptions and identify where each price or yield estimate came from. Review the plan when conditions change.'},
{id:'9-9',title:'9.9 Cost-benefit analysis',content:'Compare expected benefits with all costs. Include seed, land preparation, manure or fertiliser, labour, crop protection, harvesting, drying, bags, storage, transport, market fees and financing costs. Revenue is quantity sold multiplied by the price received. Net return is revenue minus total costs. Use conservative yield and price assumptions and update the calculation using actual records.'}
]},
{ id:'module-10', title:'Climate change', page:'60', introduction:'Climate variability affects planting, crop growth, harvest quality and markets. Climate-smart decisions combine local weather information, resilient field practices and careful post-harvest planning.', sections:[
{id:'10-1',title:'10.1 Climate change risks',content:'Bean production can be affected by irregular rainfall, longer dry spells, intense rain, flooding, warmer temperatures and changing pest and disease pressure. Risks differ by region, altitude, season and variety. Farmers should monitor local conditions and avoid assuming that past planting dates will always remain reliable.'},
{id:'10-2',title:'10.2 Climate change impacts',content:'Moisture stress can reduce emergence, flowering, pod set and grain filling. Excessive rain can cause waterlogging, erosion, disease pressure, pod rotting and harvest delays. Heat can affect flowering and pod formation. These impacts can reduce quantity, quality, household food availability and income across the value chain.'},
{id:'10-3',title:'10.3 Adaptation and mitigation across the beans value chain',content:'Adaptation means adjusting farming and business practices to reduce harm from climate impacts. Mitigation means reducing greenhouse-gas emissions or increasing carbon storage where practical. Actions may include suitable varieties, soil cover, water management, reduced residue burning, efficient transport and improved storage.'},
{id:'10-4',title:'10.4 Why adapt to climate change?',content:'Adaptation helps protect harvests, household food supply, income, soil and water resources. It can reduce losses and make production more reliable, although it cannot eliminate weather risk. Farmers should choose measures that fit their location, resources and crop system.'},
{id:'10-5',title:'10.5 Climate-smart agriculture practices on the farm',content:'Practices include using locally suitable varieties, adjusting planting dates with reliable seasonal information, rotating crops, maintaining soil cover, retaining healthy residues, reducing erosion, improving drainage, using organic matter appropriately and managing water carefully. Diversification can reduce dependence on one crop or one harvest period.'},
{id:'10-6',title:'10.6 Climate-smart post-harvest handling and storage',content:'Dry grain promptly and hygienically, protect it from rain, use suitable storage that limits moisture and pests, and monitor stored grain. Better storage reduces food loss and the need to produce replacement grain. Plan harvest labour and drying space before the season.'},
{id:'10-7',title:'10.7 Climate-smart marketing',content:'Market planning can reduce losses when weather disrupts harvest or transport. Use group bulking where it is well managed, protect grain during transport, seek market information and consider storage options that allow a better-timed sale. Understand the costs and risks of any storage or credit arrangement.'},
{id:'10-8',title:'10.8 Mitigation practices on the farm',content:'Avoid burning crop residues, maintain soil cover, manage manure and inputs efficiently, protect trees and soil where appropriate and reduce unnecessary transport and post-harvest losses. Choose practices that are practical for the farm and do not compromise food safety or crop health.'}
]}
];

export const BEANS_MANUAL_ANNEXES = [
{id:'a1',title:'Annex 1: Bean production and export trends',content:'The source manual presents historical Ugandan dry-bean production and export figures for 2010–2016. These figures describe the period covered by the manual and should not be treated as current market statistics. Use recent MAAIF, UBOS or trade data for present-day planning.'},
{id:'a2',title:'Annex 2: Biofortified bean varieties released by NARO',content:'The manual lists biofortified varieties developed to improve nutritional value, including iron-rich bean options. Availability and recommendations can change. Confirm current seed availability, variety identity and suitability with NARO, an authorised seed supplier or an extension worker.'},
{id:'a3',title:'Annex 3: National bean variety list for Uganda',content:'The source annex lists varieties, release information, altitude ranges, maturity periods, grain yield and special traits. It distinguishes bush and climbing types; varieties marked with C are climbing types that require support. Treat the printed table as a historical reference, not a current endorsement. Confirm current registration, seed availability and local suitability before choosing a variety.'},
{id:'a4',title:'Annex 4: Criteria for determining seed quality',content:'The source annex compares seed categories using criteria such as physical purity, germination, disease status, moisture, varietal purity and off-types. The values differ by category. Always check the current seed label and applicable Ugandan seed standards before accepting or rejecting a seed lot.'},
{id:'a5',title:'Annex 5: Definitions',content:'The manual defines common value-chain terms including goods, services, value chain, supply, demand and pest. In simple terms, a value chain is the linked set of people and activities that move a product from inputs and production through handling, processing and sale to the final user.'},
{id:'a6',title:'Annex 6: Farm business records',content:'Examples include a farm activity schedule, input and production records, labour records, sales records, start-up budgets, cash-flow projections and a balance sheet. Record dates, quantities, unit costs, total costs, buyers, prices and payment methods. Use the templates in the original manual as a starting point and adapt them to the farm.'},
{id:'a7',title:'Annex 7: Bean cropping calendar for Uganda',content:'The manual presents historical seasonal calendars for Northern, Eastern, South Western and Central Uganda. It notes that the first season commonly involves planting around March to mid-April and harvesting around June–July, while the second season varies by region, including later planting in the South West. Treat this as a planning reference only; use current local rainfall forecasts and extension advice.'},
{id:'a8',title:'Annex 8: Climate Change Beans Poster',content:'The source manual includes a climate-change poster summarising climate-smart practices for bean production. The poster is available in the original MAAIF PDF. Omulimi’s text adaptation highlights soil cover, water management, suitable varieties, timely planting and improved post-harvest handling.'},
{id:'a9',title:'Annex 9: References and resource guides',content:'The manual cites Ugandan and regional resources from MAAIF, NARO, CIAT, Sasakawa Global 2000, HarvestPlus, Kilimo Trust, EAC and other agricultural programmes. Refer to the original manual for the full bibliography and use current editions where available.'}
];
